\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s28 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description \
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSFormatter is an abstract class that declares an interface for objects that format the textual representation of cell contents. The Foundation framework provides two concrete subclasses of NSFormatter to generate these objects: NSNumberFormatter and NSDateFormatter. \
\fs14\
\fs28 Cells, which are instances of NSCell and its subclasses, can have any arbitrary object as their content. However, when cells are to be displayed or edited, they must convert this object to an NSString. If no formatting object is associated with a cell, the cell displays its content by invoking the localized \b description\b0  method of the object it contains. But if the cell has a formatting object, the cell invokes this object\'27s \b stringForObjectValue:\b0  method to obtain the correctly formatted string. Conversely, when the user enters text into a cell, the cell needs to convert the text to the underlying object; formatting objects handle this conversion as well.\
\fs14\
\fs28 To use a formatting object, you must create an instance of NSNumberFormatter, NSDateFormatter, or a custom NSFormatter subclass and associate the object with a cell. The cell invokes the formatting behavior of this instance every time it needs to display its object or have it edited, and every time it needs to convert a textual representation to its object. See the class description of NSDateFormatter for the details of using formatting objects.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Instances of NSFormatter subclasses are immutable. In addition, when a cell with a formatter object  is copied, the new cell retains the formatter object instead of copying it.\
\fs14\
\pard \s46 \li2310 \fi-798 \ri1007 \ql \tx2308 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Note:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 NSCell provides two methods that operate almost the same as instances of NSNumberFormatter. One method, \b setEntryType:\b0 , takes a constant that specifies  a typical numeric format (integer, float, positive float, double, and so on). With \b isEntryAcceptable:\b0 , you can ask a cell for the type of value it expects. Another method, \b setFloatingPointFormat:left:right:\b0 , allows you to specify the digits that appear to the left and right of the decimal point. See the NSCell specification for further details.\
\fs40\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Delegation Methods for Error Handling\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSControl  has delegation methods for handling errors returned in implementations of NSFormatter\'27s \b objectValue:forString:errorDescription:\b0  and \b isPartialStringValid:newEditingString:errorDescription:. \b0 These delegation methods are, respectively,\b  control:didFailToFormatString:errorDescription:\b0  and \b control:didFailToValidatePartialString:errorDescription:\b0 .\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Making a Subclass of NSFormatter\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 There are many possibilities for custom subclasses of NSFormatter. You might find use for a custom formatter of telephone numbers, or a custom formatter of part numbers.\
\fs14\
\fs28 To subclass NSFormatter, you must, at the least, override the two primitive methods s\b tringForObjectValue:\b0  and \b getObjectValue:forString:errorDescription:\b0 . In the first method you convert the cell\'27s object to a string representation; in the second method you convert the string to the object associated with the cell. \
\fs14\
\fs28 Implement \b attributedStringForObjectValue:withDefaultAttributes:\b0  in addition to \b stringForObjectValue:\b0  when the display string has attributes associated with it. For example, if you want negative financial amounts to appear in red, you would return a string with an attribute of red text. In \b attributedStringForObjectValue:withDefaultAttributes:\b0  get the non-attributed NSString by invoking \b stringForObjectValue:\b0  and then apply the proper attributes to that NSString.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If the string for editing is different than the string for display\'d0for example, the display version of a currency field should show a dollar sign but the editing version shouldn\'27t\'d0implement \b editingStringForObjectValue:\b0  in addition to \b stringForObjectValue:\b0 .\
\fs14\
\fs28 The method \b isPartialStringValid:newEditingString:errorDescription:\b0  allows you to edit the textual contents of a cell at each key press or to prevent entry of invalid characters. You might apply this on-the-fly editing to things like telephone numbers or social security numbers; the person entering data only needs to enter the number since the formatter automatically inserts the separator characters. \
\fs14\
